# Build context exclusions.
#
# Without this, `COPY . .` ships the host's node_modules (over a gigabyte on this
# machine) and Docker then fails to checksum the npm workspace symlinks, because
# Windows reports a file mode that the Linux tar writer does not recognise.
#
# Excluding node_modules here is also correct on its own terms: the image installs
# its own dependencies from package-lock.json inside the build. Copying the host
# tree would risk baking Windows-built native binaries (argon2, sharp, the Prisma
# query engine) into a Linux image, which fails at runtime rather than at build.

node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. Regenerated inside the image; copying it in would let a stale
# local build shadow a correct one.
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
.turbo
**/.turbo

# TypeScript incremental build state.
#
# The `**/` prefix is load-bearing, not decoration. Docker matches ignore
# patterns against the path relative to the context root, and a bare `*` does
# not cross directory boundaries, so `*.tsbuildinfo` matches only a file at the
# root and silently lets packages/contracts/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo through. Those
# files are what make `tsc -b` decide it is up to date, so shipping one makes the
# build emit nothing and the API then fails to resolve @naijaproperty/contracts.
# The failure is confusing precisely because the compile step "succeeded".
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets. An .env in the image would silently override the platform's injected
# environment variables, which is both a security problem and the reason the
# start script uses --env-file-if-exists.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and editor noise
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# Logs and OS cruft
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Docs are not needed to build or run.
docs
README.md
